p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Stefon Diggs</strong> (WR2) \u2014 PHI leak 42 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><p><strong>Terry McLaurin</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 PHI hold WR1s to just 52 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> is a bell-cow (77% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.81 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Dallas Goedert</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 WAS are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.35 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>DeVonta Smith</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 WAS surrender 0.51 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>PHI pound it at the goal line (36% pass in the red zone) \u2014 <strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> owns the rushing-TD equity.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to PHI \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>PHI \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>PHI are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>37%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 6-point favorite projected to lead for 40% of snaps, <strong>PHI</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>15 carries for 69 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as PHI salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>DeVonta Smith</strong> (5 targets, 49 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when PHI lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Dallas Goedert</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 51 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>WAS \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>WAS are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>38%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 WAS project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Jacory Croskey-Merritt</strong>'s rushing projection (11 carries, 50 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Terry McLaurin</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, WAS funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>6 targets for 50 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Chig Okonkwo</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teamma